[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cf7d304-cef3-55dd-b92d-e56cf5a4654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cf7d304-cef3-55dd-b92d-e56cf5a4654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594433} :answer-field/id #uuid "169bb74c-efc4-57e4-b7c5-b0045d3072a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "387e58b8-1f5a-52ef-9860-949438f59a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-336876" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{x}^{2}-20x+64}{\\sqrt{x}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=16$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{x}^{2}-20x+64}{\\sqrt{x}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{[{x}^{2}-20x+64]^{′}}{[\\sqrt{x}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{2x-20}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(16)-20}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{16}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{32-20}{(\\frac{1}{2(4)})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e27fd7f-262e-5ee5-a734-35adede96c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-t^2)}{t(t^2+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e27fd7f-262e-5ee5-a734-35adede96c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-t^2)}{t(t^2+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594406} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a3f643-3be8-5786-872f-38048e3ae4f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45164ca3-58bc-567c-b0a5-b6ced364360e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132774" :question/problem "The particle moves along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n${y}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot {(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaf90b86-ed00-521f-866e-457741256953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{(t^2+3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf90b86-ed00-521f-866e-457741256953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{(t^2+3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594566} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3de83bc-b404-5457-8f8e-eb85acb80364" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc6368e-df43-5cbe-829c-1d64f41be4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132779" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}\n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4t}{{t}^{2}+1})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-4\\int \\frac{t}{{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({t}^{2}+3)^{2})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+3)^{2}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96dafa68-a935-5a06-a7d1-49074e56dd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96dafa68-a935-5a06-a7d1-49074e56dd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594477} :answer-field/id #uuid "3238cdec-a457-52b9-b1d3-95be6ab04c89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "753ac6b8-07cd-52b3-96d2-729d969d6571" :question/math-academy-id "q-132776" :question/problem "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3-3{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}+3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6t)t({t}^{2}+3)-(3-3{t}^{2})(3{t}^{2}+3)}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}-(9-9{t}^{4})}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}-9}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{3(1)^{4}-18(1)^{2}-9}{{1}^{2}({1}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-18-9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56def86c-ab7a-5ee7-91e1-99d5dd5d5ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594412} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a4c8f1-3e83-5df0-9c6b-e3a496513aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594524} {:answer/id #uuid "cb26ec87-d4bd-5a1a-bb55-0d7023bca925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594537} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6315c5-990d-5afb-827e-1a744a823f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594538} {:answer/id #uuid "dab5e7a7-6c57-51c5-b249-2e02bfcf3c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+4}{7})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56def86c-ab7a-5ee7-91e1-99d5dd5d5ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594412} :answer-field/id #uuid "958be250-09be-539f-b9a2-68d6e58ae2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "781918da-7b76-5e94-8393-81699d8399b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80309" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7-{(2x+4)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7-(2x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7(1-(2x+4)^{2}/7)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(2x+4)^{2}/7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x+4)^{2}/7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((2x+4)/\\sqrt{7})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7-(2x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((2x+4)/\\sqrt{7})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3d72ea-fbbc-5886-8d34-b9caf1af6a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594449} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf95501-e536-5a05-83d2-fac664037521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594482} {:answer/id #uuid "ca338f45-a927-5c08-936b-604973bb92f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594534} {:answer/id #uuid "eff0b301-1118-5d44-a770-29512d4ed4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594571} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1803c7-e00d-5dca-9a57-b03334407209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eff0b301-1118-5d44-a770-29512d4ed4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594571} :answer-field/id #uuid "acbee470-9937-5a99-a6fe-91ae0d800524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c65348f-80e0-53a7-ac92-5ccaa9d54f90" :question/math-academy-id "q-96044" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{49}{49}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{6}{7} & \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} & -\\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} & -\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & -\\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{3}{7})\\cdot (7)=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105bcd11-195d-595b-a809-039508fc80c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594346} {:answer/id #uuid "1937f825-0ec1-5545-bd5d-adf5f388cea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594357} {:answer/id #uuid "d495ddb0-944a-502f-9071-272b73503df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594544} {:answer/id #uuid "ddef0704-3eda-5ac9-951f-5c99aea2177c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594552} {:answer/id #uuid "f3796a09-d461-57b4-aab5-414a49b86eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d495ddb0-944a-502f-9071-272b73503df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594544} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde2376e-c68e-5524-81ad-470a6b3cf6a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab5c1551-708e-588b-964f-08bb4b92b53c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3593" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4x+13}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+13 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+13 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+13 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4x+13}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+2)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x+2}{3}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+2}{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4x+13}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+2)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0485e8d0-a745-5f4a-b09f-9737ad49c56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2t}}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594331} {:answer/id #uuid "264e13af-97ae-55dd-8289-e0383bf64f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594373} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5631a0-edf7-5221-bb33-f425edc19c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594431} {:answer/id #uuid "c9abd531-1a5e-5993-8e8a-4187ee480a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(t+1)\\sqrt{2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594533} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e0e542-bd18-5dd9-a4b9-ae8116808bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2e0e542-bd18-5dd9-a4b9-ae8116808bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594557} :answer-field/id #uuid "3680e0fe-3e48-5354-949b-d2de364c059f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bed5f6a1-cd6e-5de8-8764-b26c3222a9a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-97611" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a particle $P$ at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=⟨t,\\,1,\\,\\sqrt{2t}⟩.$ Find the normal component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $v\\times a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨t,\\,1,\\,\\sqrt{2t}⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ t & 1 & \\sqrt{2t} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2t} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}t & \\sqrt{2t} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}t & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}},\\frac{\\sqrt{2t}}{2},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}, \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =t+1, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2t}}{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2t}+\\frac{t}{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+2t+1}{2t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{(t+1)^{2}}{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{2t}})}{t+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002a5abe-2feb-5869-a269-6a01eb2e0339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{t(t+16)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594323} {:answer/id #uuid "aa993937-7369-53c3-94a3-297dcd25611f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{t+16}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594494} {:answer/id #uuid "bb022cbe-0966-590b-aaa6-d0049a965270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t(t+16)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594516} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a43e08-f7ae-59d4-bb36-87ab6f9cc1d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t(t+4)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594545} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ca3d17-aaba-5c32-9bd1-664683912cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{\\sqrt{t+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb022cbe-0966-590b-aaa6-d0049a965270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t(t+16)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594516} :answer-field/id #uuid "009097c8-12c8-50ab-b9ed-4c6c719ed771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc6fd842-fe55-508e-aada-2552005bd218" :question/math-academy-id "q-97613" :question/problem "The position $r$ of a particle $P$ at time $t>0$ is given by $r(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},\\,4t,\\,-7⟩.$ Find the normal component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The normal component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}=\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-7)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,4,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $v\\times a,||v||$ and $||v\\times a||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\times a & =⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,4,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{t} & 4 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{t} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{t} & 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}⟩, \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{t})}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+16}, \\\\ ||v\\times a|| & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the normal component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =\\frac{||v\\times a||}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}})}{\\sqrt{t+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t(t+16)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e0db29-f8b1-5403-94b7-9ba121dcddf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{2}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594464} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1a01bd-49c1-5313-a1ef-877572cdb64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594496} {:answer/id #uuid "c1846389-328e-5dac-953c-eaf7dd057a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594522} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9a7958-b67b-5976-a8ef-1af813ef7827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+13}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594536} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4099f2-7dde-5f1b-80d1-c89860d354a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1846389-328e-5dac-953c-eaf7dd057a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594522} :answer-field/id #uuid "be095260-d43c-5ec7-a263-d22c3f81b2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1d67064-65cd-5300-8fbc-f882a54204fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-49938" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}-12x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-12x+8 & =-2({x}^{2}+6x-4) \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+[{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}]-4) \\\\  & =-2({(x+3)}^{2}-13) \\\\  & =2(13-{(x+3)}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}-12x+18}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{2(13-(x+3)^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26(1-(x+3)^{2}/13)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x+3)^{2}/13)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+3)/\\sqrt{13})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}-12x+18}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+3)/\\sqrt{13})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =11\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =11\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7556f1ab-4a0f-56f4-a312-9f26b7dbc7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7556f1ab-4a0f-56f4-a312-9f26b7dbc7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594443} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2c1ff6a-781b-54f5-a6dd-d2604cef4c2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2d0443e-1ec5-59e8-ac83-dbaed19a9bae" :question/math-academy-id "q-336885" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[ln\\,(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)})}{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(0))}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(0))})}{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4(1)}{1+0})}{2(1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0564dadc-b649-5ab0-a644-062d4ed4cc3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.4{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594333} {:answer/id #uuid "10028b91-57b0-53fc-86bf-a109f0f8bfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594345} {:answer/id #uuid "4aca9894-51a5-55aa-989d-f904e73250fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.4{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594403} {:answer/id #uuid "945eed97-e8dd-50b4-a6f3-47061c25c4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594476} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f886a3-46fa-5369-bed3-a346d02e84c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.04{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10028b91-57b0-53fc-86bf-a109f0f8bfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594345} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f7e2db-635a-5fca-82a2-b49d41d578d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5fd0466-4eb5-5461-93a9-a6cc238e9fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-5404" :question/problem "$0.\\overset{―}{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{4} & =0.444… \\\\  & =0.4+0.04+0.004+… \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{4}{100}+\\frac{4}{1\\,000}+….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series with first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{10}$ and common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{4} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\frac{1}{10}){(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0176aae3-fbd8-59e0-9df0-a3b64b394fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594824} {:answer/id #uuid "42cb5eaa-97b1-5c1c-b2af-a9814494adfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595033} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f13974-65fe-52a8-9d21-dab7f5133af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595378} {:answer/id #uuid "c15f75c2-a87d-5b36-a4b1-2d2502965c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595478} {:answer/id #uuid "dd597b33-8f38-5674-81dd-eedae7a839b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0176aae3-fbd8-59e0-9df0-a3b64b394fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594824} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb7967e-0bc0-5ce0-99ae-3b3135ec07ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f04e9c4-e396-5f9c-afd4-215de687a8b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143353" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [1,3].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3{e}^{-2x}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{e}^{-2}}{8}-(-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025e1656-6569-5f0c-9d35-4a9649c68ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594828} {:answer/id #uuid "14ee30f9-28fa-530a-8b4d-4c886737d5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594893} {:answer/id #uuid "1740426b-af3f-5e9d-8369-e133f777e9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594898} {:answer/id #uuid "22ae26c9-9596-5e48-b396-13624a4a9305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594938} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f8070c-ef96-5a04-af85-c1b407db7136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ee30f9-28fa-530a-8b4d-4c886737d5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594893} :answer-field/id #uuid "99fc683c-02f9-5242-8b60-47d1be9066c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "315fb257-a450-57a3-a9b4-d26ab05eaa97" :question/math-academy-id "q-143385" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{y+1}{3}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[x+\\frac{y{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+\\frac{y}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{y+2}{6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [0,2].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2+2x-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [0,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy)}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{(\\frac{2(x+1)}{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{y+2}{6})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements is true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bdb36c9-97e6-5733-b66e-382d5274a722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595118} {:answer/id #uuid "7321e4c0-c4a2-5e8e-921d-d6bfd265cc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595202} {:answer/id #uuid "7badb29a-3f2e-53dd-8c11-bc156c7f870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595229} {:answer/id #uuid "7de3968b-efea-54f8-b947-3926c20a0b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595234} {:answer/id #uuid "87be14cd-76f5-54b4-afc6-435935534324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7badb29a-3f2e-53dd-8c11-bc156c7f870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595229} :answer-field/id #uuid "25d57bdb-6818-5279-8bb9-16f08dc48bb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73ab8b5f-605e-5dc7-8415-ae75bef5a896" :question/math-academy-id "q-164844" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=(1-3t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [(1-3t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j]\\cdot (-3i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ (1-3t)\\cdot (-3)+2\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} & =0 \\\\ -3+9t+2 & =0 \\\\ 9t-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=\\frac{1}{9}.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{1}{9}) &=(1-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9})\\,i+2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}}\\,j=\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08911307-498e-557d-b69f-4c4a6f366299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594845} {:answer/id #uuid "7434c3a2-d090-54c9-93bb-ac56038eccc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595204} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcbbcb1-4f96-5529-81f1-009860e973c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595291} {:answer/id #uuid "981c7d7f-752a-5f9e-8826-61c574abd52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595349} {:answer/id #uuid "bd68ecf2-51d1-5121-88f9-adc4454785ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7434c3a2-d090-54c9-93bb-ac56038eccc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595204} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b1a33b-ccee-5108-91bf-b5da0f45bda8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8043a273-a319-5370-970a-271d28c684d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143585" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{3}{2},$ find $\\text{E}[2Y-4X].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}y]_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2Y-4X] & =2\\,\\text{E}[Y]+(-4)\\,\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2\\,(\\frac{3}{2})-4(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12563b7f-ad4d-5b5b-910b-0bdc5c7761ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k+u(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594884} {:answer/id #uuid "272c4bea-f1f6-572e-ba3c-425a8cfd9130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k+u(\\sqrt{2}\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594951} {:answer/id #uuid "690d70a6-6ddf-59dc-8ade-73a17128ffe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k+u(2\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595161} {:answer/id #uuid "941e67ac-18d7-5eab-a980-ce4d01903265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k+u(2\\sqrt{2}\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595331} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e0e746-263c-53eb-a287-8786268d0d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k+u(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "272c4bea-f1f6-572e-ba3c-425a8cfd9130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k+u(\\sqrt{2}\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594951} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c95a56-b351-52f3-920e-efd84fa739dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1dc177b-2fe6-5287-8ab4-4ca6361e6106" :question/math-academy-id "q-75200" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve  $r(t)=(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =(1-1)i+(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})]\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =-0\\,i+(1-1)\\,j+\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k+u(\\sqrt{2}\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186595991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b212e1d-b22b-5ced-bb6c-6f8a01c173fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-{5}^{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594917} {:answer/id #uuid "6417635c-2d9c-5fa1-a736-fc505d40234a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35[1-{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595139} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1fb811-88e4-521b-a0cc-13f4e3135521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35[{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595370} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b044f5-2099-5487-80a6-4c355741a8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{105}{2}[{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}+1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595483} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2c95a9-f392-57a2-bcb9-20938b0053d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{105}{2}[{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6417635c-2d9c-5fa1-a736-fc505d40234a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35[1-{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595139} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee573a10-63f1-5c85-8398-fbe2e228a729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186595901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc69beb8-fa80-52ca-a2c6-927443bf738a" :question/math-academy-id "q-5653" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the first term is $42$ and the common ratio is $-\\frac{1}{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=42$ and $r=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{42[1-{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}]}{1-(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{42[1-{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}]}{\\frac{6}{5}} \\\\  & =35[1-{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{N}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46374ed0-64ae-5d2f-b933-ab2ff7f6077a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46374ed0-64ae-5d2f-b933-ab2ff7f6077a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596187} :answer-field/id #uuid "04aed38e-7e3a-5a6e-8aff-eb3f05f73fa7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596491} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f0d8ffd-11de-5811-80a4-0ae15058500e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f0d8ffd-11de-5811-80a4-0ae15058500e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596230} :answer-field/id #uuid "629a71cf-289d-5587-859a-b3ef358b49be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07e9fd36-9a9c-576a-a529-46865d676a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-333864" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2xz+y,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{x-y}{{z}^{2}},\\, && z(0)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xz+y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 2+1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-y}{{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+(-\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $\\frac{7}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2$\nand\n$z(1)≈\\frac{7}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f04de77-c548-54d4-9d77-443336a358c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f04de77-c548-54d4-9d77-443336a358c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596102} :answer-field/id #uuid "d055092f-36a9-568f-ac0d-76b04c436aa1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0cd87253-f7da-5449-9edb-2587627cbed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-168008" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,f(x)=f(\\frac{5}{2})=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,{f}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{4}{5}.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})=0+{8}^{1}=8, \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))=\\frac{4}{5}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡0=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e5ce2db-8187-5fbc-81a6-433f134c78c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596262} {:answer/id #uuid "a241632d-fabb-5179-a516-2524ae1f5db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596338} {:answer/id #uuid "a436f12c-c5be-55e3-8395-65e08bd60853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596342} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a38a71-e8c7-5c8f-ab93-2bc42a62f0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596373} {:answer/id #uuid "ca57ed6f-5e0f-5568-8495-6d4b2d247ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e5ce2db-8187-5fbc-81a6-433f134c78c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596262} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2897f5-1264-55ae-8dfe-7ad7bcca64a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d00ec3f-6f5e-5fb5-9e54-e2deb62f2610" :question/math-academy-id "q-132159" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-1,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<-3.1)=0.3632,$ find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2981\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3336\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First,  we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-3.1) & =0.3632 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{-3.1-(-1)}{\\sigma }) & =0.3632 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{2.1}{\\sigma }) & =0.3632 \\\\ -\\frac{2.1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.3632)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.35)=0.3632\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.3632)=-0.35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2.1}{\\sigma } & =-0.35 \\\\ \\sigma  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00856def-8663-5367-8cf0-c4bc98d4a8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{s(s+8)}{({s}^{2}-4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596080} {:answer/id #uuid "64176ff4-586e-55bb-bc1d-757be5a75dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{s({s}^{2}+8)}{({s}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596242} {:answer/id #uuid "9b94103c-20c0-580c-9766-f9111f41a860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+8}{{s}^{4}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596325} {:answer/id #uuid "aea90755-85f2-53c8-94fe-6f8ef18cc3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596358} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bf493c-854e-54f3-9d6f-18371399278b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{s(s+8)}{({s}^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64176ff4-586e-55bb-bc1d-757be5a75dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{s({s}^{2}+8)}{({s}^{2}+4)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596242} :answer-field/id #uuid "d61ebd9e-c7f1-51b1-aea9-8aca2468a233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d0b4871-7488-5edb-9a57-3591dd91bca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-64365" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}+4y\\} & =L\\{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =4L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L\\{y\\}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{″}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{″}\\}={s}^{2}L\\{y\\}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}={s}^{2}Y(s)-s.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{″}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =4L\\{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-s)+4Y(s) & =4(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}+4)Y(s) & =s+\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{3}+8s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s({s}^{2}+8)}{({s}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201eb658-3ad1-5ea8-8cc8-fa568f2d5db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596128} {:answer/id #uuid "26f17310-0edb-542f-916a-8c57da6aec73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596140} {:answer/id #uuid "8085731e-64f5-5a67-a628-aa2d60ad8f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596284} {:answer/id #uuid "9a391520-7d49-5800-bbea-aacce057f946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596322} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7b530f-5600-5b8d-a048-83ae369570a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "201eb658-3ad1-5ea8-8cc8-fa568f2d5db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596128} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9588bb5-0c01-5ed6-9e22-37dd66972473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e52f529-60e9-51eb-91ee-90ffeab28c47" :question/math-academy-id "q-48810" :question/problem "Given that $r=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that$\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec7e5f6-9981-5cc4-bb8a-602cd53b0054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ec7e5f6-9981-5cc4-bb8a-602cd53b0054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596328} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1aeef83-2b6a-54f6-859a-fd3983f89cc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26a24488-f2a7-5cba-b390-bc6f9aa0a1ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-342059" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(1<X<5)=0.3758,$ use the table to find  $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ | $\\,0.6844\\,$ | $\\,0.6879\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ | $\\,0.7224\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ | $\\,0.7549\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $1<X<5$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =3.$*\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n$$\nP(1<X<5)=P(3-2<X<3+2)\n$$\n\nSo, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<5) & =0.3758 \\\\ 2P(3<X<5) & =0.3758 \\\\ P(3<X<5) & =0.1879 \\\\ P(X<5)-P(X<3) & =0.1879 \\\\ P(X<5)-0.5 & =0.1879 \\\\ P(X<5) & =0.6879 \\\\ P(X\\le 5) & =0.6879\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =0.6879 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{5-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.6879 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.6879 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6879)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.49)=0.6879\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6879)=0.49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.49 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.49} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈4.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a05486e8-dbd1-582f-8aa3-ed6c0b7c745c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a05486e8-dbd1-582f-8aa3-ed6c0b7c745c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596333} :answer-field/id #uuid "124878d3-32be-5de0-a602-acffbe31b445" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28d4018d-5112-5682-81ff-aad93ccb004c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342032" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<2)=0.6826,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<2)=P(-2<X<2).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2<X<2) & =0.6826 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2) & =0.6826 \\\\ P(0<X<2) & =0.3413 \\\\ P(X<2)-P(X<0) & =0.3413 \\\\ P(X<2)-0.5 & =0.3413 \\\\ P(X<2) & =0.8413 \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =0.8413\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.8413 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.8413 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.8413 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8413)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.00)=0.8413\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8413)=1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a205e7eb-7d46-5ddf-aa3d-c60a6a9f6634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a205e7eb-7d46-5ddf-aa3d-c60a6a9f6634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596337} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba696db8-d47d-5dcf-bfaa-718ab7a6fd1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a056ad97-95f4-5eb5-8942-de30003cf496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a056ad97-95f4-5eb5-8942-de30003cf496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596334} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7ef418-0699-5977-a6dd-5f25b544683e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ea9c90d-6c8d-58d5-89bf-e85d3c6ee680" :question/math-academy-id "q-334181" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=3x-y+z,\\, && y(1)=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=2x-3y-z,\\, && z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{7}{2})$ and $z\\,(\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\n$\\,y\\,(\\frac{7}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z\\,(\\frac{7}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=\\frac{7}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7}{2}-1=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-y+z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2+(-2) \\\\  & =3-2-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x-3y-z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2-(-2) \\\\  & =2-6+2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ |  | $-2$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+(-\\frac{5}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+(-5)=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{7}{2})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$z\\,(\\frac{7}{2})≈-7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b97f31b-c76b-5402-8b78-a625badf2318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596151} {:answer/id #uuid "b9949fd7-4297-5dc2-b24b-4d1afc8bb2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596377} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c6e6e8-88a7-5b59-9437-3ac5413ba5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596394} {:answer/id #uuid "dd03e02e-d8d1-5fc7-bce6-50f0c48fc7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596436} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f6b37d-80ae-5137-94ae-937ff0c0ae2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b97f31b-c76b-5402-8b78-a625badf2318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "center: $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ radius: $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596151} :answer-field/id #uuid "7782e322-974d-545c-9618-66f1728d5e36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47a6c6a4-7176-591f-8d3c-111699d2923e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76371" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}+y=\\frac{7}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}+y & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}+y) & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =\\frac{7}{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-(-\\frac{1}{2}))}^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba0775a7-e448-5bfc-9d53-51c59fa48ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0775a7-e448-5bfc-9d53-51c59fa48ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596378} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e062458-98f4-5725-ab24-0a8daa738770" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edb7d11c-86d3-504e-80e7-6ba4891f8a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb7d11c-86d3-504e-80e7-6ba4891f8a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596460} :answer-field/id #uuid "c360d72a-699b-5299-bb95-96f241531f28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53a902c6-9f13-5252-b1e2-952857757b3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335120" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=x+y+z,\\, && y(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=y-z,\\, && z(-1)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=0$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=0$ and $z(-1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y+z=(-1)+0+1=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y-z=0-1=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y+z=(-\\frac{1}{2})+0+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y-z=0-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈0\\,\\text{and}\\,z(0)≈\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1cd8e9-d89f-5538-bcce-5b5b47c34c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596120} {:answer/id #uuid "55895a45-4235-5703-a263-cc06bc5e4344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{4}$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596215} {:answer/id #uuid "730d49fb-18a6-552e-bc9a-1fb7926810e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{16}$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596266} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef8ffe9-d771-570d-b7a4-d4f72aa74556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596305} {:answer/id #uuid "91aeb0d9-32f6-586b-ba83-fd2f65a59e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730d49fb-18a6-552e-bc9a-1fb7926810e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{16}$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596266} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb5eda4-403d-5947-b4bb-71b6d7def693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eeb0a22-29f1-5e6d-a8fa-948985f7f3b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15074" :question/problem "![](images/27/276497b6f3ed88f51e3b08eef008ac5e2340748b73628d187d90c750ca68684e.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=1$ units. What is the largest possible area $A$ of the circular sectors?" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta$ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $1$ unit, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n1=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{1-2r}{r}=\\frac{1}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{1}{r}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=\\frac{1}{2}-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the largest area will have a radius of $\\frac{1}{4}$, and the area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4})-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d315f58e-2f3c-560f-9f1f-77c594742661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d315f58e-2f3c-560f-9f1f-77c594742661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596413} :answer-field/id #uuid "d61f42b3-d6ef-58c4-a94a-44eacfecef19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9979f237-ee91-5238-852f-85e56821efe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342717" :question/problem "Given that $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot [{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-2{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})] \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5bc088d-7144-5105-911a-e17022356a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5bc088d-7144-5105-911a-e17022356a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596346} :answer-field/id #uuid "af5cd78b-3449-52a3-9b9e-e1477615d63e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aa671d8-1ad1-5313-b555-8b912938119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aa671d8-1ad1-5313-b555-8b912938119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596300} :answer-field/id #uuid "b109cfac-f333-5924-abdd-6784f1ef6f5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aab77ec-a0db-5d9f-9983-9e7cd4cecf05" :question/math-academy-id "q-335121" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2x-z,\\, && y(-1)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=x+y,\\, && z(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=0$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=1$ and $z(-1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-z=2\\cdot (-1)-0=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+y=-1+1=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-z=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-0=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+y=-\\frac{1}{2}+0=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+(-\\frac{1}{4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(0)≈-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(0)≈-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124fec83-1f69-5fe2-8fd5-2df9c55d99c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596108} {:answer/id #uuid "37dd5156-ae3d-5b37-af13-dbe0f6937ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596171} {:answer/id #uuid "45e0eab2-6e46-5846-be99-1d6feccccb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596186} {:answer/id #uuid "7185a322-8672-5610-a9b5-c23c5fd4e99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596264} {:answer/id #uuid "8a27c8d3-ed47-5d10-97c4-e018d7bc00a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e0eab2-6e46-5846-be99-1d6feccccb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596186} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d19a2c-81d0-5d9f-ae2f-1da903dd1656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad90f299-0817-5b56-8c4a-5ec599ed8e9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-102639" :question/problem "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi$ intersects the line $x-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7bed7dd-1a61-553f-b7be-30bec2ade2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7bed7dd-1a61-553f-b7be-30bec2ade2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596474} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f95f2aa-11fe-54ab-85d9-428407f968a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bec4a874-a41b-518a-a92a-6eedad8e8888" :question/math-academy-id "q-345085" :question/problem "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-y(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-3-Y(s)=-6(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-3-Y(s) & =-6(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =3-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =\\frac{3({s}^{2}-1)}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>1.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+1}\\} \\\\ y(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccd84be-44fd-5957-9e12-09a076519888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ccd84be-44fd-5957-9e12-09a076519888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596203} :answer-field/id #uuid "a95c5904-99b3-50c7-a9e4-7da29e0b8e1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d00fb409-3734-5353-87c2-f962b119c15e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342072" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(1,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-2<X<4)=0.6424,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-2<X<4$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =1.$*\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2<X<4) & =0.6424 \\\\ 2P(1<X<4) & =0.6424 \\\\ P(1<X<4) & =0.3212 \\\\ P(X<4)-P(X<1) & =0.3212 \\\\ P(X<4)-0.5 & =0.3212 \\\\ P(X<4) & =0.8212 \\\\ P(X\\le 4) & =0.8212\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =0.8212 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{4-1}{\\sigma }) & =0.8212 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) & =0.8212 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8212)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.92)=0.8212\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8212)=0.92.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =0.92 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{0.92} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3.260\\,87.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} &=(3.260\\,87)^{2}≈10.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05797707-c769-5946-a36f-086842d69359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596085} {:answer/id #uuid "80def5f3-46a5-599c-9bb3-285a89c3a85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596288} {:answer/id #uuid "937a3c33-70da-5b35-b526-ab7879621b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596310} {:answer/id #uuid "d987e24f-037e-5cd7-bebb-6c71603843d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596430} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5281b2-b163-5d05-aa9e-f4e3ff5152e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "937a3c33-70da-5b35-b526-ab7879621b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596310} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd767394-85cf-51b5-87b4-0ec56f5638db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5521701-8621-502f-b0ac-86704fb98331" :question/math-academy-id "q-121027" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={630}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=30.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cffac52-c9c6-577f-b097-550809c72ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596096} {:answer/id #uuid "231f3988-9434-5502-b13e-c6b8d93de359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596135} {:answer/id #uuid "5871b775-d6a2-56ef-bb59-03b1117b13cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596219} {:answer/id #uuid "7de4664f-a48b-57ff-ac55-3e414bcc06f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596281} {:answer/id #uuid "fd296ff0-7b5c-5b6f-8f57-9be2858fe0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de4664f-a48b-57ff-ac55-3e414bcc06f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596281} :answer-field/id #uuid "9086eb67-561f-5b6f-97df-2fd226bd2280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186596567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbdb0348-d18b-5ac6-9c7a-ff7ece785eeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-15784" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=1-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-2x|).\n$$"}]
